#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f26ac8fd-24e5-5b0f-a3f2-f32c565b92d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275437} {:answer/id #uuid "a753eb55-eda9-5819-8868-27e498f2f52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275438} {:answer/id #uuid "e66f5fd5-c87c-5d87-b241-8da731327115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275439} {:answer/id #uuid "89b3a5a4-aa1f-5236-96ba-aa2fbd44aa86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275440} {:answer/id #uuid "30b76ce2-9d08-5ead-8903-e2bbd5860202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f26ac8fd-24e5-5b0f-a3f2-f32c565b92d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275437} :answer-field/id #uuid "b44355b5-dcdc-57e1-86c4-4af0a4f69770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ae4888-9f4e-5350-a6dc-7fdb91edbc7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115736" :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-2,4].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $22.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $22$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+16 & =22 \\\\ 2k & =22-16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1e61861-8ff6-5e8d-b394-aee184004b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275444} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f77e0b-032d-577c-adde-d2eb73981d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e61861-8ff6-5e8d-b394-aee184004b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275444} :answer-field/id #uuid "85488c99-3ebe-5fd1-b862-8f9193f3487d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2a1eaff-a513-598a-8f28-b6c62e22c235" :question/math-academy-id "q-346442" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table above gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-1,11]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $96$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)+f(7)+f(11)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+k+6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4k+32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $96,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k+32 & =96 \\\\ 4k & =96-32 \\\\ k & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ k & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32a2d1d-abc7-5036-b42d-6acdab958a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275448} {:answer/id #uuid "61aa31d3-f7d8-5311-bfa1-14c4e61efbab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275449} {:answer/id #uuid "d330d367-42c2-5cf4-bd05-dbbb64f422cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275450} {:answer/id #uuid "64ed92e1-33fe-57b7-82a6-303906358490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64ed92e1-33fe-57b7-82a6-303906358490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275451} :answer-field/id #uuid "40bcf070-a64e-5990-808a-a2db0461c83d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275447} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9d55312-c3f1-577c-ac17-8843b915060a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275453} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bde09c-1fe9-5959-9b8f-5572d9198d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5bde09c-1fe9-5959-9b8f-5572d9198d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275454} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdaf32a6-3d32-5093-ba32-071bf63269ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2321c850-8d6e-5cdb-b1d2-aa0aa4eba56a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275456} {:answer/id #uuid "372694fc-ab4f-5700-a70e-724aeabc781e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275457} {:answer/id #uuid "c56eb15b-ef97-52ce-9db2-fe4a2d8a8c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "exact value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "372694fc-ab4f-5700-a70e-724aeabc781e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275457} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1f5d297-2d85-55e8-8686-c1c67cd8f3f9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "277725a3-237e-5051-ae07-4598fad2f8f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-346467" :question/problem "Consider the area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$The estimated area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/assets/1cb4c2c69c93117bd5eed39322059a3c0af84d16b293ccfc053c677ac0b6127d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2.5)+f(3)+f(3.5)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $25$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+16+9+4)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =54\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{decreasing}$ over the interval $[2,4],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc1cfce2-6c67-5656-87bc-be24f48eda93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275461} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbba9c1-d208-5e0d-9a78-34e013b77432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275462} {:answer/id #uuid "dc228cb9-000b-5a1b-be3b-3059a46fc7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275463} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f63acf-8db0-5178-8632-91127264c441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275464} {:answer/id #uuid "650f6364-88bd-507c-acb9-321b25deaf37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc1cfce2-6c67-5656-87bc-be24f48eda93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275461} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2a99f58-21b0-5f70-be82-6f1b49360c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db39980f-8283-5ef8-83b5-f354ae931bda" :question/math-academy-id "q-74348" :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(5.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(8)Δ{x}_{2}+f(9)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1.5+6\\cdot 2.5+9.5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6+15+9.5 \\\\  & =30.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94e554a5-29ef-5d6a-9778-0f1c7aaa6c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275468} {:answer/id #uuid "adc3ca3f-6f32-5621-aeca-a53b4820ba3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275469} {:answer/id #uuid "da45de20-2c71-5cc4-a07b-43b3e6924efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275470} {:answer/id #uuid "4b11eb67-5db6-584c-bae9-3d6d0d845c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275471} {:answer/id #uuid "1b06fac7-46cf-532f-beb1-a72504162ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94e554a5-29ef-5d6a-9778-0f1c7aaa6c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275468} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e270a1-0c6c-5922-ad57-e71feba6121f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb378625-ff34-5649-9eb8-1bcb7d6bde31" :question/math-academy-id "q-86350" :question/problem "The following table shows the velocity of a plane during a $20$-minute trip.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{kilometers/minute})$ | $17$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance traveled during this period has a value of  $450\\,\\text{km}$, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v(5)+v(10)+v(15)+v(20)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+27+k+18)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5(k+70) \\\\  & =5k+350\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled during the $20$ minute period. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $450\\,\\text{km},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+350 & =450 \\\\ 5k & =450-350 \\\\ k & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
